1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:14,001
Hand tools, older than man himself, crafted from chipped rocks and cherry red steel.

2
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:20,840
Now, hand tools on Modern Marvels.

3
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Tools, one syllable, five letters.

4
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:50,360
A simple word that can refer to virtually every human achievement,

5
00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:54,120
a countless number of objects, and even our own evolution.

6
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:56,380
Hi, I'm Ron Hazleton.

7
00:00:56,760 --> 00:00:58,060
Welcome to the Tool Bench.

8
00:00:58,061 --> 00:00:59,520
You know, when I was growing up,

9
00:00:59,521 --> 00:01:01,940
I used to play in my dad's workshop.

10
00:01:01,941 --> 00:01:04,300
Tools were like toys for me and my brother.

11
00:01:04,301 --> 00:01:08,440
We spent countless hours making soapbox derby cars.

12
00:01:08,441 --> 00:01:11,040
Today, I help people all across the country

13
00:01:11,041 --> 00:01:13,380
with do-it-yourself home improvement projects.

14
00:01:14,340 --> 00:01:16,680
I'm always struck by the special kind of bonding

15
00:01:16,681 --> 00:01:18,640
that occurs when a family uses tools

16
00:01:18,641 --> 00:01:21,340
to make something in their lives better.

17
00:01:21,341 --> 00:01:23,680
And I guess it shouldn't come as a surprise.

18
00:01:23,681 --> 00:01:27,160
Tools have been a vital part of the human experience.

19
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:30,960
December 12, 1998.

20
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:34,300
Astronauts Jerry Ross and James Newman disembark the space

21
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:35,800
shuttle Endeavour.

22
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:39,740
Their destination is Unity, the recently installed American

23
00:01:39,840 --> 00:01:43,880
made portion of the International Space Station.

24
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:48,180
250 miles above the Earth, Ross and Newman attach a space

25
00:01:48,280 --> 00:01:50,140
age toolbox to Unity.

26
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:53,980
Inside are more than 50 hand tools, wrenches, ratchets,

27
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:57,720
and clamps, which will be used in at least 150 future

28
00:01:57,721 --> 00:02:02,460
to complete the construction of the space station.

29
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:04,960
These tools are similar to those used on Earth,

30
00:02:05,060 --> 00:02:06,520
with a few differences.

31
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:09,660
Handles are wider, since the bulky gloves worn by the

32
00:02:09,760 --> 00:02:13,260
astronauts make it difficult to grip narrower objects.

33
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:16,980
And most importantly, each tool is equipped with a ring so

34
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:20,080
that it can be attached to the astronaut's suit, preventing

35
00:02:20,180 --> 00:02:21,580
it from floating away.

36
00:02:21,581 --> 00:02:25,580
away.

37
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:28,580
Tools are building a future in space just as they've built

38
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:30,580
human civilization back on Earth.

39
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:37,820
These are the very first tools, chipped stones that are at least

40
00:02:37,920 --> 00:02:41,840
2 and 1 half million years old, older than the human species

41
00:02:41,940 --> 00:02:43,340
itself.

42
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:47,980
In fact, it's possible that tool making helped give rise

43
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:49,740
to humanity.

44
00:02:49,741 --> 00:03:03,741
Our brains, the type of teeth we have, the sophistication in our hands is all directly related to this 2.5 million year legacy of natural selection for tool use.

45
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:15,800
A husband and wife team of anthropologists, Nicholas Toth and Kathy Schick, see a link between tool making and human evolution.

46
00:03:15,801 --> 00:03:24,040
It really does seem that tools have made us what we are, that it transformed us from

47
00:03:24,041 --> 00:03:34,920
a rather insignificant-looking, bipedal, ape-like creature on the African landscape into living

48
00:03:34,921 --> 00:03:39,200
in the kind of world that we live in today.

49
00:03:39,201 --> 00:03:44,420
The first toolmakers were at least three evolutionary generations removed from modern

50
00:03:44,421 --> 00:03:45,680
humans.

51
00:03:45,681 --> 00:03:48,880
They included a species called Homo habilis.

52
00:03:48,881 --> 00:03:51,840
His name means handyman.

53
00:03:51,841 --> 00:03:54,980
Habilis lived in East Africa and stood about 3 and 1

54
00:03:54,981 --> 00:03:56,720
half feet tall.

55
00:03:56,721 --> 00:04:00,320
Primarily an herbivore, he had large teeth

56
00:04:00,321 --> 00:04:02,720
for grinding down plants.

57
00:04:02,721 --> 00:04:07,140
His brain was about 1 third the size of a modern human's.

58
00:04:07,141 --> 00:04:09,080
But that was big enough to figure out

59
00:04:09,081 --> 00:04:11,880
that by striking two stones together,

60
00:04:11,881 --> 00:04:16,880
we could create a cutting tool with a razor sharp edge.

61
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:21,620
Some people see these very earliest stone tools

62
00:04:21,621 --> 00:04:23,120
and they say, well how do you really know

63
00:04:23,121 --> 00:04:25,560
these aren't just naturally broken rocks?

64
00:04:25,561 --> 00:04:26,900
How do you know they're really artifacts

65
00:04:26,901 --> 00:04:28,900
produced by ancient humans?

66
00:04:28,901 --> 00:04:31,140
I could find something like this in my backyard.

67
00:04:31,141 --> 00:04:32,440
And my answer to that is,

68
00:04:32,441 --> 00:04:34,700
if you can really find something like this in your backyard,

69
00:04:34,701 --> 00:04:37,000
you probably have an archeological site

70
00:04:37,001 --> 00:04:39,240
because rocks don't break like this in nature.

71
00:04:39,241 --> 00:04:40,900
You can see patterning there.

72
00:04:40,901 --> 00:04:44,400
You can see intent on the part of the makers on what they're doing.

73
00:04:44,401 --> 00:04:51,060
Schick and Toth often demonstrate how habilis used these tools to become a meat-eater.

74
00:04:51,061 --> 00:04:55,900
And whenever they do, they only use animals that have died of natural causes.

75
00:04:55,901 --> 00:05:09,700
Today it's an Indian gazelle acquired from a zoo.

76
00:05:09,701 --> 00:05:12,440
They begin by striking the stones in the same manner

77
00:05:12,540 --> 00:05:13,600
habilis would have.

78
00:05:19,940 --> 00:05:22,740
Once a sharp edge is formed, they go to work.

79
00:05:25,940 --> 00:05:29,260
Since tools of this size would be difficult to fell a large,

80
00:05:29,360 --> 00:05:33,000
fast-moving animal with, Homo habilis was more a scavenger

81
00:05:33,100 --> 00:05:33,900
than a hunter.

82
00:05:33,901 --> 00:05:42,180
But this ability to quickly butcher dead animals before the hyenas got to them provided an

83
00:05:42,181 --> 00:05:50,660
entirely new food source, a key element for survival and evolution.

84
00:05:50,661 --> 00:05:58,940
Besides cutting tools, Homo habilis also used rocks to create hammers and anvils for cracking

85
00:05:58,941 --> 00:06:01,840
nut shells and animal bones.

86
00:06:01,841 --> 00:06:09,300
You normally try to do it in a way where you're creating a crack that goes down the long axis of the bone rather than straight across.

87
00:06:10,020 --> 00:06:14,380
Inside the bones, they found an excellent source of protein, marrow.

88
00:06:16,520 --> 00:06:22,900
Then you can open it up easily and remove the marrow, which is essentially a tube of food.

89
00:06:22,901 --> 00:06:32,340
More than providing a good meal, the ability of our pre-human ancestors to use tools might have actually sparked their evolution.

90
00:06:32,960 --> 00:06:38,600
I don't think it's an accident that starting right after the origins of stone tools,

91
00:06:38,700 --> 00:06:46,740
we start seeing the first evidence of brain expansion, the first evidence of a decrease in the size of teeth through time as well.

92
00:06:46,741 --> 00:06:53,480
So you don't need these massive jaws and massive teeth to process food because you're using tools to do that instead.

93
00:06:54,100 --> 00:06:57,620
It frees you from your biological heritage.

94
00:06:58,400 --> 00:07:01,980
You can supplement your biology by the use of technology.

95
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:11,040
Stone tools would slowly continue to evolve with only a couple of noticeable advances.

96
00:07:11,041 --> 00:07:14,980
First, about a million and a half years ago,

97
00:07:15,340 --> 00:07:17,940
came the appearance of larger, flatter tools.

98
00:07:18,680 --> 00:07:22,040
They had a wide cutting edge and were referred to as hand axes.

99
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:27,340
Then, about half a million years ago, the tools got smaller again,

100
00:07:27,760 --> 00:07:32,120
but they were much more refined and had finely serrated cutting edges.

101
00:07:33,140 --> 00:07:35,920
Think of them as Stone Age steak knives.

102
00:07:35,921 --> 00:07:42,920
When fully modern human beings evolved about 100,000 years ago, they shared the earth with

103
00:07:42,921 --> 00:07:46,920
the slope-browed species Neanderthal.

104
00:07:46,921 --> 00:07:52,920
It was at this time that one of the greatest advances in tool technology occurred, the

105
00:07:52,921 --> 00:07:59,920
use of a compound tool, tying one tool, a rock, to another tool, a stick, creating a

106
00:07:59,921 --> 00:08:04,920
new, stronger tool.

107
00:08:04,921 --> 00:08:09,160
About 6,500 years ago, the Stone Age would come to an end,

108
00:08:09,660 --> 00:08:11,880
and toolmaking would change dramatically.

109
00:08:13,980 --> 00:08:16,060
Mesopotamians living in Jordan and Israel

110
00:08:16,061 --> 00:08:17,540
made an important discovery.

111
00:08:18,760 --> 00:08:21,360
When certain colorful rocks were heated

112
00:08:21,361 --> 00:08:22,860
to the point of disintegration,

113
00:08:23,480 --> 00:08:26,520
they would produce a malleable substance, copper.

114
00:08:28,120 --> 00:08:30,580
Copper could be shaped and molded,

115
00:08:30,581 --> 00:08:33,400
and when it cooled, was stronger than stone.

116
00:08:34,920 --> 00:08:41,500
It wasn't hard to figure out, once they were able to get these funny colored rocks to melt in a very hot fire,

117
00:08:42,020 --> 00:08:47,740
and out came this stuff that they could use to make swords and axe heads and things of that nature.

118
00:08:47,741 --> 00:08:55,840
It wasn't very far behind that they started using the copper for everything, including saws and so forth.

119
00:08:56,720 --> 00:09:04,000
By the time those ultimate do-it-yourselfers, the Egyptians, began building the pyramids almost 5,000 years ago,

120
00:09:04,001 --> 00:09:07,700
many of today's common workbench tools were in use.

121
00:09:08,400 --> 00:09:11,500
While wooden mallets and stone hammers were used

122
00:09:11,501 --> 00:09:13,880
during the thousand years of pyramid construction,

123
00:09:14,820 --> 00:09:18,060
Egyptian blacksmiths used both copper and bronze

124
00:09:18,061 --> 00:09:21,980
to make saws, chisels, hand drills,

125
00:09:22,340 --> 00:09:25,660
and a unique axe-like tool called an axe.

126
00:09:26,980 --> 00:09:29,980
In about 1100 B.C., it was discovered

127
00:09:29,981 --> 00:09:32,980
that a mineral called hematite could also produce

128
00:09:32,981 --> 00:09:35,280
and even stronger metal, iron.

129
00:09:36,360 --> 00:09:40,240
But to do so, it had to be heated in excess of 500 degrees.

130
00:09:41,080 --> 00:09:45,160
To do this, oxygen was used to stoke the fire to hotter temperatures.

131
00:09:47,060 --> 00:09:52,200
Iron would be the primary source of tools from the Roman Empire through the 19th century.

132
00:09:58,260 --> 00:10:01,780
The volunteer carpenters of Habitat for Humanity

133
00:10:01,781 --> 00:10:05,620
build homes all over the world for families in need.

134
00:10:07,780 --> 00:10:10,620
They're often joined by President Jimmy Carter

135
00:10:10,720 --> 00:10:13,020
and his wife, Roseland.

136
00:10:13,120 --> 00:10:16,020
The Carters have dedicated much of their post-White House

137
00:10:16,120 --> 00:10:18,020
years to this work.

138
00:10:18,120 --> 00:10:21,540
But whether you're a former world leader or a shop clerk,

139
00:10:21,640 --> 00:10:24,540
you've probably driven a nail or pulled a nail

140
00:10:24,640 --> 00:10:27,840
without giving much thought to the tool you used.

141
00:10:27,940 --> 00:10:30,480
You might not have known, but the hammer

142
00:10:30,481 --> 00:10:32,980
is the king of tools.

143
00:10:33,080 --> 00:10:35,480
It reminds me of something a poet said,

144
00:10:35,580 --> 00:10:38,980
by hammer in hand all the arts do stand.

145
00:10:39,080 --> 00:10:42,420
And I think that alludes to why people would call the hammer

146
00:10:42,520 --> 00:10:44,020
the king of tools.

147
00:10:44,120 --> 00:10:47,440
If you think of creation as forging something,

148
00:10:47,540 --> 00:10:49,740
pounding something out, it really

149
00:10:49,840 --> 00:10:53,340
evokes the image of a basic hammer.

150
00:10:53,440 --> 00:10:57,780
And all throughout history, we see just dozens and dozens

151
00:10:57,781 --> 00:11:01,780
and dozens of different variations on the basic hammer.

152
00:11:01,781 --> 00:11:06,020
Hammers have been used to build our homes, tenderize our meat,

153
00:11:06,021 --> 00:11:08,540
and even test our health.

154
00:11:08,541 --> 00:11:11,120
But none is more common than the claw hammer.

155
00:11:11,121 --> 00:11:13,180
This is a very old tool.

156
00:11:13,181 --> 00:11:15,820
In fact, it could have been used by a world leader who

157
00:11:15,821 --> 00:11:18,240
lived a long time before Jimmy Carter.

158
00:11:18,241 --> 00:11:21,420
Try Julius Caesar.

159
00:11:21,421 --> 00:11:23,540
These are actual claw hammer heads

160
00:11:23,541 --> 00:11:28,540
during the Roman Empire more than 2,000 years ago.

161
00:11:28,640 --> 00:11:31,740
This hammer style would endure for centuries.

162
00:11:31,840 --> 00:11:34,180
There was really only one thing wrong with it.

163
00:11:34,280 --> 00:11:36,780
The head would fly off.

164
00:11:36,880 --> 00:11:39,780
Flying hammerheads was a problem that would baffle

165
00:11:39,880 --> 00:11:42,000
hammer makers until the 19th century.

166
00:11:45,800 --> 00:11:48,300
Now, this hammer was fashioned very much

167
00:11:48,400 --> 00:11:52,900
after the Roman style, which was the very deep arc

168
00:11:52,901 --> 00:11:57,300
to the back and the claw so that nails could be pulled.

169
00:11:57,400 --> 00:12:00,200
This is not a Roman hammer.

170
00:12:00,300 --> 00:12:04,840
This is about early 18th century.

171
00:12:04,940 --> 00:12:10,320
The head itself had a hole that was perfectly straight

172
00:12:10,420 --> 00:12:12,860
and the head would fall off many times.

173
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:16,420
And what they did was try to improve that

174
00:12:16,520 --> 00:12:21,860
by putting a strap forged right to the actual head

175
00:12:21,861 --> 00:12:24,860
one on either side.

176
00:12:24,861 --> 00:12:28,860
Straphead hammers were common throughout the 18th century,

177
00:12:28,861 --> 00:12:31,860
but they still didn't solve the problem.

178
00:12:31,861 --> 00:12:34,860
The straps were too thin to handle much pounding

179
00:12:34,861 --> 00:12:37,860
and just snapped at the head as it flew off.

180
00:12:37,861 --> 00:12:43,860
It wasn't until 1840 that a New York blacksmith, David Maydoll,

181
00:12:43,861 --> 00:12:45,860
devised a solution.

182
00:12:45,861 --> 00:12:49,860
He was inspired by the design of another ancient tool.

183
00:12:49,861 --> 00:12:54,860
tool, the adze.

184
00:12:54,960 --> 00:12:59,260
An adze is a wood planing tool that dates back to the Egyptians

185
00:12:59,360 --> 00:13:03,200
and is still used by craftsmen today.

186
00:13:03,300 --> 00:13:06,200
Madol, who often repaired the broken hammers of local

187
00:13:06,300 --> 00:13:10,220
carpenters, noticed that the adze had a deep tapered socket

188
00:13:10,320 --> 00:13:13,720
where the blade attached to the handle.

189
00:13:13,820 --> 00:13:16,320
He adapted this concept to the hammer

190
00:13:16,321 --> 00:13:20,860
and made what has become known as the ad socket hammer.

191
00:13:20,960 --> 00:13:24,520
The deeper socket was key to securing the head.

192
00:13:24,620 --> 00:13:26,360
Other carpenters discovered these hammers.

193
00:13:26,460 --> 00:13:28,060
And all of a sudden, David Madall

194
00:13:28,160 --> 00:13:30,460
wasn't just a small town blacksmith any longer.

195
00:13:30,560 --> 00:13:32,460
He was in the hammer production business.

196
00:13:32,560 --> 00:13:33,980
And that's what he did.

197
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:36,880
Interestingly enough, he never patented his design

198
00:13:36,980 --> 00:13:39,180
for the ad socket for the hammer.

199
00:13:39,280 --> 00:13:41,720
And it's something I'm sure he lived to regret,

200
00:13:41,820 --> 00:13:45,420
because I'm sure he could have died a wealthy man instead

201
00:13:45,421 --> 00:13:51,620
of a humble hammer maker. Over the centuries, the hammer took

202
00:13:51,621 --> 00:13:55,576
on several other interesting shapes and designs.

203
00:13:55,577 --> 00:13:59,120
During colonial times, iron tools were rare

204
00:13:59,121 --> 00:14:04,060
in America, since the British wouldn't import any to their unruly expatriates.

205
00:14:07,060 --> 00:14:12,420
So when iron wasn't available, a tree trunk would make an effective sledgehammer.

206
00:14:12,421 --> 00:14:19,040
In stark contrast were these Victorian-era domestic hammers.

207
00:14:19,760 --> 00:14:24,460
Nickel-plated steel was used to create delicate-looking and incredibly ornate tools.

208
00:14:25,280 --> 00:14:29,780
They were quite popular in proper households for cracking nut shells.

209
00:14:31,140 --> 00:14:36,240
In 1902, George Voigt took out a patent on the double-claw hammer.

210
00:14:36,880 --> 00:14:40,800
The second claw provided greater leverage for extracting nails.

211
00:14:40,801 --> 00:14:47,800
But since the lower claw had to be forged separately, it was weak and often broke.

212
00:14:47,801 --> 00:14:52,800
Mittendorfer Strauss patented this goat head hammer in 1928.

213
00:14:52,801 --> 00:14:58,800
While it wasn't much of a heavy-duty tool, the long ears made for an amusing nail puller.

214
00:14:58,801 --> 00:15:05,800
The hammer was used as a promotional item inside boxes of Billy Boy soap during the Depression.

215
00:15:05,801 --> 00:15:11,740
While this cast iron fish head hammer has a certain charm lacking in today's tools,

216
00:15:12,360 --> 00:15:17,580
modern hammers possess other qualities that tool makers of the past could never have imagined.

217
00:15:19,280 --> 00:15:24,540
I kind of seriously doubt if the average person looks at anything as simple as a hammer

218
00:15:24,541 --> 00:15:28,620
and realizes that the amount of work that has gone into every detail of that.

219
00:15:28,621 --> 00:15:36,160
This is one of the hammer development stations at the Stanley Tool Plant in New Britain,

220
00:15:36,161 --> 00:15:37,153
Connecticut.

221
00:15:37,154 --> 00:15:43,440
This combination of electronic sensors, digital technology and human muscle,

222
00:15:43,441 --> 00:15:51,640
is used to produce a hammer that will minimize the impact on the user's hand, wrist and arm.

223
00:15:51,641 --> 00:15:55,232
When a muscle is in motion, it gives off an electrical signal.

224
00:15:55,233 --> 00:15:56,680
These sensors can analyze

225
00:15:56,681 --> 00:16:05,160
that signal and graphically display on a computer the impact of every swing and strike.

226
00:16:05,161 --> 00:16:10,960
This concern for how a tool impacts the body is summed up by the buzzword of the tool industry

227
00:16:10,961 --> 00:16:14,000
today, ergonomics.

228
00:16:14,001 --> 00:16:20,100
In practice, ergonomics means using technology to make tools that do minimal or no damage

229
00:16:20,101 --> 00:16:21,540
to our bodies.

230
00:16:21,541 --> 00:16:26,680
A carpenter would swing a hammer all of his life, God knows how many thousands of

231
00:16:26,681 --> 00:16:30,960
times a day and never realized that each one of those blows

232
00:16:30,961 --> 00:16:33,280
is slowly doing something to his body

233
00:16:33,281 --> 00:16:34,760
because it wasn't like one accident.

234
00:16:34,761 --> 00:16:36,880
If he fell off the roof and broke his arm,

235
00:16:36,881 --> 00:16:38,320
he'd know that he broke his arm.

236
00:16:38,321 --> 00:16:40,560
But if after 20 years of swinging a hammer,

237
00:16:40,561 --> 00:16:43,800
he suddenly has trouble moving that arm,

238
00:16:43,801 --> 00:16:47,680
well, he's not going to be sure exactly what it was.

239
00:16:47,681 --> 00:16:51,400
But it probably was a cumulative trauma disorder.

240
00:16:51,401 --> 00:16:54,300
Impact testing showed the Stanley team something

241
00:16:54,301 --> 00:17:01,300
Shock waves from a normal hammer were transmitted through the arm, similar to the vibration

242
00:17:01,301 --> 00:17:02,881
of a tuning fork.

243
00:17:02,882 --> 00:17:09,300
Since the goal was to find a way of shortening and numbing that shock

244
00:17:09,301 --> 00:17:17,300
wave, they decided to put a tuning fork in the hammer and let it absorb the shock.

245
00:17:17,301 --> 00:17:20,712
Literally put a tuning fork into the handle of the hammer.

246
00:17:20,713 --> 00:17:22,300
And what it does is, as the

247
00:17:22,301 --> 00:17:25,940
comes through the shaft, it goes into the tuning fork

248
00:17:26,040 --> 00:17:28,540
and vibrates it, and it absorbs the shock.

249
00:17:28,640 --> 00:17:31,840
And therefore, it doesn't get into the arm and into the hand.

250
00:17:31,940 --> 00:17:34,740
In 1998, the tuning fork hammer

251
00:17:34,840 --> 00:17:39,060
was officially named the Stanley Antivybe.

252
00:17:39,160 --> 00:17:41,780
And it went into production at the company's Shelbyville,

253
00:17:41,880 --> 00:17:43,260
Tennessee hammer plant.

254
00:17:43,261 --> 00:17:44,260
hammer plant.

255
00:17:49,660 --> 00:17:53,300
The anti-vibe starts as a small rolled bar of steel

256
00:17:53,400 --> 00:17:55,200
called a billet.

257
00:17:55,300 --> 00:17:57,680
The billet is fed through an induction heater where

258
00:17:57,780 --> 00:18:02,280
temperatures range between 2,200 and 2,300 degrees

259
00:18:02,380 --> 00:18:03,840
Fahrenheit.

260
00:18:03,940 --> 00:18:06,980
In just a few seconds, the glowing red-hot billet

261
00:18:07,080 --> 00:18:07,980
exits the heater.

262
00:18:13,260 --> 00:18:23,600
That incredibly loud noise is the sound of a 1,300-ton press forging billets into hammerheads.

263
00:18:29,540 --> 00:18:38,200
Four magnificent blows from the press, and instantly the billet has been shaped into not one, but a pair of hammerheads.

264
00:18:38,201 --> 00:18:47,200
Our forging process is similar to what the blacksmith forging process was, say, 100, 200 years ago.

265
00:18:47,660 --> 00:18:55,440
We do in four blows what a blacksmith probably used 100, 200 blows to do to form a hammerhead.

266
00:18:57,500 --> 00:19:00,620
After forging, an important detail is added.

267
00:19:01,320 --> 00:19:06,360
The face of the hammer is placed over a grinding wheel that produces a checkered surface.

268
00:19:06,361 --> 00:19:12,460
That checker face is there to make sure that the hammer does not slip off the nails.

269
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:20,560
Next, the hammer heads go through a battery of finishing and assembly stations.

270
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,220
The first stop is at the Whirlybird.

271
00:19:24,780 --> 00:19:29,740
Here the face and chapper, or circular surface, around the face are polished.

272
00:19:29,741 --> 00:19:38,740
A conveyor belt moves the heads from the whirlybirds to the automated robots.

273
00:19:38,741 --> 00:19:51,340
These robots are precisely programmed to polish each of the octagonal surfaces connected to the

274
00:19:51,341 --> 00:19:58,340
chapper.

275
00:20:02,180 --> 00:20:07,500
Finally, actual human beings run each hammerhead on belt polishers to bring a finish to the

276
00:20:07,501 --> 00:20:13,500
top and sides.

277
00:20:13,501 --> 00:20:16,500
Next, they enter a steel enclosure.

278
00:20:16,501 --> 00:20:21,120
Once inside, they pass under the piercing light of a laser beam.

279
00:20:21,121 --> 00:20:32,040
This is how the Stanley logo is emblazoned onto the hammer.

280
00:20:32,041 --> 00:20:37,160
From the laser station, the heads are placed on racks where they're coated with lacquer

281
00:20:37,161 --> 00:20:40,720
for long-term protection from corrosion.

282
00:20:40,721 --> 00:20:43,960
The lacquer is applied by electrostatic guns.

283
00:20:43,961 --> 00:20:49,940
Like a magnet, this technology uses electrically charged particles to draw the lacquer spray

284
00:20:49,941 --> 00:20:53,480
to the tool.

285
00:20:53,481 --> 00:20:57,240
Finally, the actual tuning fork is inserted into the head.

286
00:21:03,520 --> 00:21:06,380
A powerful epoxy, one that was originally created

287
00:21:06,381 --> 00:21:10,420
for the aerospace industry, is used to secure the connection.

288
00:21:13,160 --> 00:21:16,000
Now, the grip is ready to be added.

289
00:21:16,001 --> 00:21:19,900
The process begins with this box of polyvinyl chloride,

290
00:21:19,901 --> 00:21:22,340
or PVC, pellets.

291
00:21:22,341 --> 00:21:25,720
The pellets are sucked out of the box by a vacuum hose.

292
00:21:25,721 --> 00:21:27,940
They're fed into an injection molding machine

293
00:21:27,941 --> 00:21:30,560
where they're heated to a liquid state

294
00:21:30,561 --> 00:21:32,240
and molded around the handle.

295
00:21:37,140 --> 00:21:40,340
Finally, the Anti-Vibe is ready to pound some steel.

296
00:21:40,341 --> 00:21:46,340
While the anti-vibes basic shape harkens back to the Romans,

297
00:21:46,341 --> 00:21:49,340
its design reaches toward the future.

298
00:21:49,341 --> 00:22:01,340
Now make no mistake, I love my power tools.

299
00:22:01,341 --> 00:22:06,340
But I have to admit, there's a certain satisfaction in accomplishing something with a hand tool.

300
00:22:06,341 --> 00:22:11,020
tool. And for thousands of craftsmen and hobbyists, there's something especially

301
00:22:11,021 --> 00:22:16,340
meaningful about restoring and using vintage hand tools.

302
00:22:16,341 --> 00:22:23,260
Many vintage tools were made not only to last, but to be beautiful. Author and

303
00:22:23,261 --> 00:22:28,240
furniture maker Shondor Natsalansi photographs such tools for publication

304
00:22:28,241 --> 00:22:35,260
in his books, including the art of fine tools. He finds a value in vintage tools

305
00:22:35,261 --> 00:22:39,760
that's lacking in today's mass-produced tools.

306
00:22:39,860 --> 00:22:42,060
There's a really special delight in picking up

307
00:22:42,160 --> 00:22:46,300
a particularly well-made tool, one that just resonates

308
00:22:46,400 --> 00:22:48,000
when it's in your hands.

309
00:22:48,100 --> 00:22:50,800
It's no different, really, than picking up a guitar or a violin

310
00:22:50,900 --> 00:22:52,220
and making beautiful music.

311
00:22:56,320 --> 00:22:58,520
For woodworkers like Natsalanzi,

312
00:22:58,620 --> 00:23:01,420
few tools evoke a greater sense of artistry

313
00:23:01,520 --> 00:23:03,660
than the hand plane.

314
00:23:03,661 --> 00:23:07,900
This simple tool is used to smoothen and finish wood surfaces.

315
00:23:07,901 --> 00:23:10,900
Other variations can create grooves and cabinets

316
00:23:10,901 --> 00:23:14,700
and perform numerous other woodworking jobs.

317
00:23:14,701 --> 00:23:18,700
Its basic design has been the same for the past 2,000 years.

318
00:23:22,620 --> 00:23:24,820
Like the claw hammer, its earliest use

319
00:23:24,821 --> 00:23:26,420
can be traced to the Romans.

320
00:23:29,060 --> 00:23:31,260
Throughout history, there have been thousands

321
00:23:31,261 --> 00:23:34,960
of minor personal touches added to this basic design

322
00:23:34,961 --> 00:23:37,500
by countless craftsmen.

323
00:23:37,501 --> 00:23:41,200
Because of this, the hand plane is the most popular tool

324
00:23:41,201 --> 00:23:43,700
amongst collectors.

325
00:23:43,701 --> 00:23:46,340
Its invention was a revolutionary advance

326
00:23:46,341 --> 00:23:50,820
in precision hand tools.

327
00:23:50,821 --> 00:23:54,620
Its predecessor, the ads, was effective at creating planks

328
00:23:54,621 --> 00:23:58,060
from round, rough logs.

329
00:23:58,061 --> 00:24:01,700
But the plane could remove paper-thin slices of wood,

330
00:24:02,100 --> 00:24:03,580
leaving a fine finish.

331
00:24:07,440 --> 00:24:11,220
The plane is like a chisel with a special housing built around it

332
00:24:11,221 --> 00:24:14,360
for increased control, power, and accuracy.

333
00:24:15,200 --> 00:24:18,440
Like a shoe, a hand plane has a sole or flat surface

334
00:24:18,441 --> 00:24:20,300
that is pushed over a piece of wood.

335
00:24:21,420 --> 00:24:24,960
A small slit in the sole allows for the protrusion of an iron

336
00:24:24,961 --> 00:24:26,700
or steel adjustable blade.

337
00:24:26,701 --> 00:24:28,700
Simple, right?

338
00:24:28,701 --> 00:24:30,700
Wrong.

339
00:24:30,701 --> 00:24:33,700
It can be remarkably difficult to use.

340
00:24:33,701 --> 00:24:37,700
It takes quite a bit of know-how and technique

341
00:24:37,701 --> 00:24:39,700
to set up a hand plane properly

342
00:24:39,701 --> 00:24:44,700
and then to use it correctly to get a beautiful smooth surface.

343
00:24:44,701 --> 00:24:48,700
With a plain blade, you're actually going to slice through

344
00:24:48,701 --> 00:24:51,700
those wood fibers to get a nice clean smooth surface.

345
00:24:51,701 --> 00:24:54,700
So that blade has to be very, very sharp.

346
00:24:54,701 --> 00:25:00,760
And then the amount of the blade protruding beyond the sole has to be just right.

347
00:25:01,220 --> 00:25:06,800
That controls the degree of cut or the size or the thickness of the shaving that the plane's going to produce.

348
00:25:07,900 --> 00:25:12,680
And then there's the question of being able to create an even smooth surface on the tabletop,

349
00:25:12,780 --> 00:25:14,340
which involves quite a bit of technique.

350
00:25:16,260 --> 00:25:20,520
Planing is such a precision art that the publisher of Fine Woodworking Magazine

351
00:25:20,521 --> 00:25:24,000
has produced an entire how-to video on the subject.

352
00:25:24,700 --> 00:25:27,740
How you stand even affects how the tool works.

353
00:25:29,140 --> 00:25:30,060
How does it feel?

354
00:25:30,100 --> 00:25:30,800
Are you comfortable?

355
00:25:31,180 --> 00:25:32,160
Yeah, it feels pretty good.

356
00:25:32,780 --> 00:25:34,900
It looks a little wide to me.

357
00:25:35,260 --> 00:25:36,960
Bring your legs a little bit closer together.

358
00:25:37,540 --> 00:25:46,020
The irony of such a simply designed tool being so complicated to use is just one thing that makes planes so interesting.

359
00:25:47,600 --> 00:25:54,020
They're also one of the first tools that had the toolmaker's name engraved into them, beginning in the early 1800s.

360
00:25:54,700 --> 00:25:57,700
These were men who didn't make their living making tools,

361
00:25:57,800 --> 00:26:02,600
but instead had to make their own tools to make a living.

362
00:26:02,700 --> 00:26:05,440
They were also men who took pride in their tools,

363
00:26:05,540 --> 00:26:07,940
and often made them a work of fine art

364
00:26:08,040 --> 00:26:10,640
as much as anything else.

365
00:26:10,740 --> 00:26:15,560
Some were crafted from rare materials like rosewood, ivory,

366
00:26:15,660 --> 00:26:18,160
and ebony.

367
00:26:18,260 --> 00:26:20,060
Some were whimsical.

368
00:26:20,061 --> 00:26:27,060
Planes from other parts of the world reflect certain cultural traits or spiritual principles.

369
00:26:27,061 --> 00:26:34,060
Japanese wood planes, like the one owned by tool collector Curtis Bowden, are never pushed.

370
00:26:34,061 --> 00:26:34,981
They're pulled.

371
00:26:34,982 --> 00:26:41,060
Pulling the plane towards the body reflects a Japanese philosophy of bringing all things to center.

372
00:26:41,061 --> 00:26:48,060
The spirit of the wood is brought to the craftsmanship.

373
00:26:48,061 --> 00:26:51,060
The spirit of the wood is brought to the craftsman,

374
00:26:51,160 --> 00:26:53,060
rather than being pushed away.

375
00:26:58,900 --> 00:27:01,900
Out of the thousands of craftsmen who have made planes,

376
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:04,920
perhaps the most famous was an American cabinetmaker

377
00:27:05,020 --> 00:27:08,220
named Leonard Bailey.

378
00:27:08,320 --> 00:27:10,820
In the mid-1800s, Bailey developed a number of

379
00:27:10,920 --> 00:27:15,020
improvements that are still incorporated into planes today.

380
00:27:15,021 --> 00:27:18,020
resulted in a more precise depth adjuster

381
00:27:18,120 --> 00:27:20,920
and better mechanisms for securing the blade.

382
00:27:24,020 --> 00:27:28,760
In 1879, Bailey's company, along with his numerous patents,

383
00:27:28,860 --> 00:27:32,100
was purchased by one of the emerging big tool corporations

384
00:27:32,200 --> 00:27:35,040
of the industrial age, Stanley Tools.

385
00:27:37,640 --> 00:27:43,540
Stanley still manufactures and sells Bailey planes today.

386
00:27:43,541 --> 00:27:46,500
But the relationship between Leonard Bailey and Stanley

387
00:27:46,600 --> 00:27:50,980
Tools was not always an amicable one.

388
00:27:51,080 --> 00:27:54,380
His understanding was he had a certain percent of royalties

389
00:27:54,480 --> 00:27:56,180
for every plane made.

390
00:27:56,280 --> 00:27:58,980
Stanley wanted to make planes other than the ones designed

391
00:27:59,080 --> 00:28:02,500
by Leonard, and there got to be disputes about what Leonard's

392
00:28:02,600 --> 00:28:05,100
royalties were based on.

393
00:28:05,200 --> 00:28:09,600
Leonard Bailey left Stanley in 1875 and began making

394
00:28:09,700 --> 00:28:12,200
a new line of planes.

395
00:28:12,201 --> 00:28:16,440
He called them Victor and Defiance.

396
00:28:16,540 --> 00:28:18,740
It's commonly held that he chose those names

397
00:28:18,840 --> 00:28:21,540
as a snub to Stanley.

398
00:28:21,640 --> 00:28:24,740
The tool company was unperturbed.

399
00:28:24,840 --> 00:28:27,980
They continued manufacturing the original Bailey planes

400
00:28:28,080 --> 00:28:32,520
and had a covetous eye on Bailey's new planes.

401
00:28:32,620 --> 00:28:36,020
He was a good plane manufacturer.

402
00:28:36,120 --> 00:28:39,200
He had some very, very powerful patents.

403
00:28:39,300 --> 00:28:40,720
He was not a good businessman.

404
00:28:40,721 --> 00:28:45,420
He tried a couple of times and did not do well on his own.

405
00:28:45,520 --> 00:28:47,520
Before the century was out, Stanley

406
00:28:47,620 --> 00:28:49,960
would acquire the Victor and Defiance lines

407
00:28:50,060 --> 00:28:53,760
from Leonard Bailey as well, and continued manufacturing

408
00:28:53,860 --> 00:28:58,380
the two planes through the 1950s.

409
00:28:58,480 --> 00:29:00,380
After the reacquisition of his planes,

410
00:29:00,480 --> 00:29:04,280
Bailey left the business altogether.

411
00:29:04,380 --> 00:29:05,880
He moved to Weatherfield, Connecticut

412
00:29:05,980 --> 00:29:08,380
and became a successful copy press designer.

413
00:29:10,720 --> 00:29:20,140
But he has left behind a name that will live on, not only in tool history books, but on the shelves of hardware stores and woodworking shops.

414
00:29:24,440 --> 00:29:30,280
Some of the world's rarest tools can be found in this seven-story castle in Doylestown, Pennsylvania.

415
00:29:31,680 --> 00:29:33,620
It's home to the Mercer Museum.

416
00:29:33,621 --> 00:29:37,620
A Harvard Law School graduate turned amateur archaeologist

417
00:29:37,621 --> 00:29:43,620
named Henry Mercer completed the museum in 1916.

418
00:29:43,621 --> 00:29:46,620
Mercer felt it was important to preserve the everyday tools

419
00:29:46,621 --> 00:29:50,620
used throughout history.

420
00:29:50,621 --> 00:29:53,620
It's here that tool collector and author Herbert Keene

421
00:29:53,621 --> 00:29:58,620
demonstrates how many of these tools were made and used.

422
00:29:58,621 --> 00:30:02,620
The intrigue was how in the world could you make anything

423
00:30:02,621 --> 00:30:10,860
anything out of such rudimentary objects such as these tools 100, 200 years ago, and how

424
00:30:10,861 --> 00:30:14,200
did they work? At the Mercer Museum, we're reminded

425
00:30:14,201 --> 00:30:19,773
that tools like cordless drills are really not new at all.

426
00:30:19,774 --> 00:30:22,560
In fact, cordless drills have

427
00:30:22,561 --> 00:30:24,891
been around since the Stone Age.

428
00:30:24,892 --> 00:30:28,680
The first drills, simple sharpened sticks, were made

429
00:30:28,681 --> 00:30:31,740
tens of thousands of years ago.

430
00:30:31,741 --> 00:30:34,620
This 2,000-year-old Native American drill

431
00:30:34,621 --> 00:30:37,120
represented a technological leap

432
00:30:37,121 --> 00:30:41,060
because it attached a bit to the stick, in this case,

433
00:30:41,061 --> 00:30:41,860
an arrowhead.

434
00:30:44,640 --> 00:30:46,780
A short time later, somebody figured out

435
00:30:46,781 --> 00:30:50,060
that wrapping the cord of a bow around the stick

436
00:30:50,061 --> 00:30:54,640
could make drilling less strenuous and more effective.

437
00:30:54,641 --> 00:30:57,860
This very refined example from the 1800s

438
00:30:57,861 --> 00:31:00,260
makes the drill look like a cousin to the violin.

439
00:31:03,220 --> 00:31:05,560
While bow drills are beautiful to look at,

440
00:31:05,561 --> 00:31:07,360
they're not very efficient because

441
00:31:07,361 --> 00:31:09,380
of their reciprocal motion.

442
00:31:09,381 --> 00:31:14,000
They drill on one stroke and then reset on the next.

443
00:31:14,001 --> 00:31:16,760
This medieval era breast drill solved that problem

444
00:31:16,761 --> 00:31:19,960
by providing for consistent motion.

445
00:31:19,961 --> 00:31:21,640
But as demonstrated here, it relied

446
00:31:21,641 --> 00:31:24,860
on the weight of the user and made drilling holes

447
00:31:24,861 --> 00:31:25,600
quite a workout.

448
00:31:27,860 --> 00:31:32,860
One of the best drill designs can be traced back to the 1500s.

449
00:31:32,960 --> 00:31:36,460
The bit brace might have been inspired by the crook of a tree

450
00:31:36,560 --> 00:31:38,600
branch.

451
00:31:38,700 --> 00:31:42,600
This bit brace from the early 1800s was used in a shipyard,

452
00:31:42,700 --> 00:31:46,520
and its precision craftsmanship is evident.

453
00:31:46,620 --> 00:31:52,520
This piece, being almost 200 years old and still has the

454
00:31:52,521 --> 00:31:56,020
has the precision of the fit.

455
00:31:56,021 --> 00:32:01,120
It clicks in by virtue of two end pieces that are shaped

456
00:32:01,121 --> 00:32:02,720
like the end of a clothespin.

457
00:32:02,721 --> 00:32:08,220
It will not come out under utmost pressure.

458
00:32:08,221 --> 00:32:10,420
Like hand planes, many bit braces

459
00:32:10,421 --> 00:32:12,820
came to be elaborately decorated,

460
00:32:12,821 --> 00:32:15,320
sources of pride for their makers.

461
00:32:15,321 --> 00:32:17,520
And they were not only tools of industry,

462
00:32:17,521 --> 00:32:20,520
but tools of medicine.

463
00:32:20,521 --> 00:32:23,780
You look at some of the braces that collectors have on their shelves,

464
00:32:23,781 --> 00:32:26,840
and you think, oh, here's a lovely little bit brace

465
00:32:26,841 --> 00:32:29,380
that maybe was used for more delicate drilling

466
00:32:29,381 --> 00:32:30,600
because it's only this big.

467
00:32:30,860 --> 00:32:32,160
And then the collector will say,

468
00:32:32,340 --> 00:32:34,600
actually, that's a surgeon's brace.

469
00:32:34,840 --> 00:32:37,200
And you realize that what it was used for

470
00:32:37,201 --> 00:32:39,200
was drilling holes in the skull.

471
00:32:41,000 --> 00:32:42,840
With the Industrial Revolution,

472
00:32:43,200 --> 00:32:44,920
drill technology advanced.

473
00:32:45,580 --> 00:32:47,620
Gear drills resemble egg beaters,

474
00:32:47,621 --> 00:32:50,620
but are capable of performing heavy-duty work.

475
00:32:51,560 --> 00:32:55,020
Bronze and nickel-plated gears attached to a sturdy crank handle.

476
00:32:55,700 --> 00:32:58,420
The user could apply body weight to the top of the drill

477
00:32:58,421 --> 00:33:00,080
for additional boring power.

478
00:33:02,680 --> 00:33:05,260
While vintage drills and other tools

479
00:33:05,261 --> 00:33:07,560
abound in museum and private collections,

480
00:33:08,380 --> 00:33:11,140
finding old saws is another story.

481
00:33:12,160 --> 00:33:13,640
They were entirely used up.

482
00:33:13,641 --> 00:33:15,760
In fact, a couple of craftsmen that I visited

483
00:33:15,761 --> 00:33:22,180
had only a couple of old handsaw handles that had been fitted with modern blades.

484
00:33:22,320 --> 00:33:25,380
They no longer had their original blades because they had been used up.

485
00:33:26,820 --> 00:33:32,320
Before the 20th century, saw blades were always one of the most difficult tools to make.

486
00:33:33,100 --> 00:33:37,760
Creating one with the delicate combination of hardness, stiffness, and flexibility

487
00:33:37,761 --> 00:33:41,500
was a challenge met only by the most elite metal workers.

488
00:33:41,501 --> 00:33:45,500
Then, repeated sharpening by the tool's owner just wore the

489
00:33:45,600 --> 00:33:49,500
blades all the way down to the handle.

490
00:33:49,600 --> 00:33:52,740
But most saw blades would just buckle and break before they

491
00:33:52,840 --> 00:33:54,740
could ever be worn down.

492
00:33:59,160 --> 00:34:03,260
For heavy-duty demands such as logging, two-man operated frame

493
00:34:03,360 --> 00:34:06,500
pit saws were used.

494
00:34:06,600 --> 00:34:10,700
These were first used during the 1700s as recreated by the

495
00:34:10,701 --> 00:34:14,580
created by these craftsmen at Colonial Williamsburg.

496
00:34:14,581 --> 00:34:16,840
Tree trunks or large wood planks

497
00:34:16,841 --> 00:34:19,320
would be laid across a deep pit.

498
00:34:19,321 --> 00:34:21,360
One worker would stand above the pit,

499
00:34:21,361 --> 00:34:23,340
while the other stood in it.

500
00:34:23,341 --> 00:34:28,120
The sawing was done vertically rather than horizontally.

501
00:34:28,121 --> 00:34:31,200
The idea was pretty good, as long as the bottom man

502
00:34:31,201 --> 00:34:36,300
was wearing his hat to catch the falling sawdust.

503
00:34:36,301 --> 00:34:39,620
The quality of saw blades would increase in the 19th century

504
00:34:39,621 --> 00:34:43,260
thanks to the use of steel.

505
00:34:43,261 --> 00:34:47,960
In 1855, a Philadelphia toolmaker named Henry Diston

506
00:34:47,961 --> 00:34:51,860
created the first steel melting plant in America.

507
00:34:51,861 --> 00:34:58,060
He also built a special rolling mill exclusively for saw blades.

508
00:34:58,061 --> 00:35:03,040
These machines would unfurl strips of steel, cut them to size,

509
00:35:03,041 --> 00:35:06,380
and stamp them with saw teeth.

510
00:35:06,381 --> 00:35:11,720
The rolling mill went into operation around 1862.

511
00:35:11,721 --> 00:35:15,880
Americans were able to buy domestically made handsaws.

512
00:35:15,881 --> 00:35:19,560
And Diston became kind of the father of the handsaw in America.

513
00:35:19,561 --> 00:35:23,960
And Diston is still a brand name in America today.

514
00:35:23,961 --> 00:35:26,560
Variations of Henry Diston's rolling mill

515
00:35:26,561 --> 00:35:31,080
are still used today to manufacture saw blades.

516
00:35:31,081 --> 00:35:34,580
Huge coils of steel ground to several hundredths of an inch

517
00:35:34,581 --> 00:35:38,080
thickness snake their way through powerful presses.

518
00:35:41,920 --> 00:35:43,860
There, they are cut to proper length

519
00:35:43,861 --> 00:35:45,820
and for the desired row of teeth.

520
00:35:50,300 --> 00:35:53,760
While the blades and the handles are produced by machines,

521
00:35:53,761 --> 00:35:56,600
the assembly is still done by hand at most tool plants.

522
00:36:00,300 --> 00:36:03,940
While this device may give new meaning to the word power saw,

523
00:36:04,580 --> 00:36:07,880
It's actually one of the ways saws are tested.

524
00:36:07,881 --> 00:36:10,640
This machine, found at the Stanley Tool Plant,

525
00:36:10,641 --> 00:36:13,460
counts how many strokes a particular saw requires

526
00:36:13,461 --> 00:36:15,140
to cut through a piece of wood.

527
00:36:16,160 --> 00:36:20,620
A combination of ingenuity, technology, and quality testing

528
00:36:20,621 --> 00:36:23,440
has resulted in a generation of hand saws

529
00:36:23,441 --> 00:36:25,740
that can cut through nearly anything.

530
00:36:32,300 --> 00:36:33,220
You want a book?

531
00:36:33,221 --> 00:36:34,400
Go to the library.

532
00:36:34,401 --> 00:36:36,320
You want a tool, go to the hardware store.

533
00:36:36,640 --> 00:36:40,180
But if you want a book about hardware stores, then talk to Vince Staton.

534
00:36:40,620 --> 00:36:45,020
He grew up working in his dad's hardware store, then wrote a book about these gathering places

535
00:36:45,021 --> 00:36:49,360
where the talk, the sounds, and the smells are all about tools.

536
00:36:52,300 --> 00:36:56,640
I think a hardware store has a definite smell and a definite sound.

537
00:36:59,360 --> 00:37:03,340
You come into a place and it's sort of got paint, and it's got burnt motor oil,

538
00:37:03,341 --> 00:37:06,920
and it's got sawdust and glue and all the things they do in here.

539
00:37:07,420 --> 00:37:11,240
And I guess if you had to encapsulize it in one word, it would be the smell of work.

540
00:37:11,920 --> 00:37:14,040
And you also have the sounds of work.

541
00:37:14,120 --> 00:37:16,480
And sometimes the sounds of work can actually be cheerful

542
00:37:16,481 --> 00:37:21,740
as the people are talking, asking questions about how you do this, how you do that.

543
00:37:23,800 --> 00:37:25,160
How much am I going to owe you?

544
00:37:25,740 --> 00:37:29,380
Oh, today, about 32 cents.

545
00:37:29,840 --> 00:37:33,300
You're sort of all a part of a hardware store community.

546
00:37:33,340 --> 00:37:36,840
and the meetings seemed to convene every Saturday morning.

547
00:37:37,840 --> 00:37:39,560
Throughout much of the 20th century,

548
00:37:39,700 --> 00:37:43,320
the hardware store was not only a place to learn how to use tools,

549
00:37:43,840 --> 00:37:48,440
but in Staten's case, it was an inspiration to learn the stories behind the tools.

550
00:37:49,560 --> 00:37:52,000
This is for serious work here.

551
00:37:52,500 --> 00:37:56,220
This thing weighs a lot, costs a lot too, $33.99.

552
00:37:57,100 --> 00:38:02,340
He was especially intrigued by a plumbing tool invented around 1850

553
00:38:02,341 --> 00:38:05,380
that has become known as the Monkey Wrench.

554
00:38:05,800 --> 00:38:07,340
He actually titled his book...

555
00:38:07,920 --> 00:38:09,840
Did Monkeys Invent the Monkey Wrench?

556
00:38:09,940 --> 00:38:11,640
You know, it'd be a great story if they did.

557
00:38:12,320 --> 00:38:13,660
So, who did?

558
00:38:14,320 --> 00:38:18,260
According to Staten, Solomon Merrick owned a machine shop

559
00:38:18,261 --> 00:38:21,500
in Springfield, Massachusetts in the mid-1800s.

560
00:38:22,260 --> 00:38:24,260
One of his employees, John White,

561
00:38:24,360 --> 00:38:27,400
was working on ways to improve a British-made tool

562
00:38:27,401 --> 00:38:30,140
called the adjustable spanner wrench.

563
00:38:30,141 --> 00:38:35,140
Rumor has it that something about White's physical appearance earned him the nickname

564
00:38:35,141 --> 00:38:41,140
Monkey. Whenever anyone in the shop needed to borrow the wrench he was working on...

565
00:38:41,141 --> 00:38:43,722
They would say, hey, can I use Monkey's wrench?

566
00:38:43,723 --> 00:38:46,140
And that's how the adjustable spanner wrench

567
00:38:46,141 --> 00:38:48,931
became to be known as the Monkey wrench.

568
00:38:48,932 --> 00:38:52,140
There are other stories found in the aisles of

569
00:38:52,141 --> 00:38:53,625
hardware stores as well.

570
00:38:53,626 --> 00:38:58,140
You wouldn't think making a tool as simple as a shovel could be an act of

571
00:38:58,141 --> 00:39:01,940
treason, but for an American colonist, it was.

572
00:39:02,620 --> 00:39:08,500
The colonies really were originally just supposed to be a market for British goods.

573
00:39:09,140 --> 00:39:16,300
And there was a guy named John Ames who started making shovels in 1774, which meant that was

574
00:39:16,301 --> 00:39:17,550
an act of treason.

575
00:39:17,551 --> 00:39:22,620
Specifically, Ames committed treason because he was making shovels out of

576
00:39:22,621 --> 00:39:23,321
metal.

577
00:39:23,322 --> 00:39:28,680
During the colonial years, the British forbade the manufacture of metal tools and

578
00:39:28,681 --> 00:39:31,266
would not export any to the colonies.

579
00:39:31,267 --> 00:39:34,620
So men like Ames defiantly set up their own tool

580
00:39:34,621 --> 00:39:38,060
making operations. The British would only export wooden

581
00:39:38,061 --> 00:39:39,651
shovels to this country.

582
00:39:39,652 --> 00:39:43,960
So John Ames said that his metal shovels did damage to the earth,

583
00:39:43,961 --> 00:39:48,020
whereas the wooden shovels were damaged by the earth.

584
00:39:48,021 --> 00:39:54,560
When John Ames used to make these back in colonial days, he came up with two improvements.

585
00:39:54,561 --> 00:39:56,880
One was what's called the Ames bend.

586
00:39:56,881 --> 00:39:59,520
Before that, it was just straight up and down.

587
00:39:59,521 --> 00:40:04,400
And he figured that out because that made it a whole lot easier on people's backs with

588
00:40:04,401 --> 00:40:06,320
just that little bend.

589
00:40:06,321 --> 00:40:12,340
And also he started tempering the blades, which made them thinner and more flexible

590
00:40:12,341 --> 00:40:15,580
and easier to go into the earth.

591
00:40:15,581 --> 00:40:20,300
Another famous hardware name was born during the Industrial Revolution.

592
00:40:20,301 --> 00:40:26,180
In 1843, Frederick Stanley began a business in New Britain, Connecticut, but he wasn't

593
00:40:26,181 --> 00:40:28,640
making tools.

594
00:40:28,641 --> 00:40:32,940
The Stanley Bolt Manufactory made wrought iron bolts and hinges.

595
00:40:32,941 --> 00:40:38,120
Stanley is sort of the name in hinges and hinge hardware.

596
00:40:38,121 --> 00:40:43,180
And the reason is because Stanley, when he started this company, I mean, lots of people

597
00:40:43,181 --> 00:40:44,180
were making hinges.

598
00:40:44,181 --> 00:40:46,880
These hinges were pretty much like everybody else's.

599
00:40:46,881 --> 00:40:52,860
He got the bright idea to package the screws in with the hinge.

600
00:40:52,861 --> 00:40:53,860
His took off.

601
00:40:53,861 --> 00:40:58,560
He became the name in hinges.

602
00:40:58,561 --> 00:41:03,820
Selling hinges and screws together in clearly labeled boxes made Frederick Stanley very

603
00:41:03,821 --> 00:41:05,320
wealthy.

604
00:41:05,321 --> 00:41:10,840
It also sparked the growth of one of the world's largest tool companies.

605
00:41:10,841 --> 00:41:17,840
In 1920, Stanley Bolt Manufactory merged with a company owned by a distant cousin of Frederick's.

606
00:41:17,841 --> 00:41:23,840
The Stanley Rule and Level Company started out selling boxwood and ivory levels and rulers

607
00:41:23,841 --> 00:41:29,840
before adding several other tools to their line.

608
00:41:29,841 --> 00:41:37,840
The company grew rapidly by buying out the patents of innovative but under-financed independent toolmakers.

609
00:41:37,841 --> 00:41:40,500
A lot of these companies, they were very small.

610
00:41:40,501 --> 00:41:43,740
They didn't have the horsepower to sell the product,

611
00:41:43,741 --> 00:41:47,400
but they had a good idea and they had smart people.

612
00:41:47,401 --> 00:41:50,560
And Stanley made a number of acquisitions

613
00:41:50,561 --> 00:41:53,720
to get a hold of the product line

614
00:41:53,721 --> 00:41:56,880
and the patent protection, and in several cases,

615
00:41:56,881 --> 00:42:00,960
the people behind that so that they could continue

616
00:42:00,961 --> 00:42:04,340
working for Stanley.

617
00:42:04,341 --> 00:42:06,540
The patents of plane maker Leonard Bailey

618
00:42:06,541 --> 00:42:09,540
some of their most successful acquisitions.

619
00:42:11,880 --> 00:42:14,780
With future acquisitions, Stanley would go on to produce

620
00:42:14,880 --> 00:42:19,280
just about every hand tool imaginable.

621
00:42:19,380 --> 00:42:22,280
Today's mass-produced tools might lack the beauty

622
00:42:22,380 --> 00:42:26,300
and individuality of earlier tools,

623
00:42:26,400 --> 00:42:30,160
but they are marvels of technology built on the shoulders

624
00:42:30,260 --> 00:42:33,440
of those early tool makers who worked so hard

625
00:42:33,441 --> 00:42:35,784
hard to make life easier.

626
00:42:35,785 --> 00:42:42,440
And while an innovation such as inserting a tuning fork into the handle

627
00:42:42,441 --> 00:42:50,440
of a hammer might beckon toward the future, it also connects us to our prehistoric past.

628
00:42:50,441 --> 00:42:56,647
With our ancestors, we first made a tool from chipped stone.

629
00:42:56,648 --> 00:42:59,440
We share a key human trait.

630
00:42:59,441 --> 00:43:09,440
Our desire and maybe even a drive to figure things out, figure how to do something better,

631
00:43:09,441 --> 00:43:15,440
figure how to alter something in order to make something work differently,

632
00:43:15,441 --> 00:43:21,440
I think that goes deep in our prehistoric past, that it has very, very deep roots.

633
00:43:21,441 --> 00:43:25,440
And now we've amplified it to the nth degree.

634
00:43:29,440 --> 00:43:34,200
All these incredible advances in technology not only make tools easier to use,

635
00:43:34,560 --> 00:43:37,660
but they give us more free time for doing the things that we love,

636
00:43:38,100 --> 00:43:40,380
like working with tools.

637
00:43:41,140 --> 00:43:43,320
For Modern Marvels, I'm Ron Hazleton.

